<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Trinity</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Trinitas, Trinitarianism</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Trinity is the Christian doctrine that one God exists in three divine persons, God the Father, God the Son (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus Christ</a>) and God the Holy Spirit, held to be coeternal and to share one essence. Early Christians and the fathers of the Church developed it from the late 2nd century onwards, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-council-of-nicaea">First Council of Nicaea</a> and the First Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in the 4th century established it as part of the creed. Most major Christian denominations today are Trinitarian. Those who reject the doctrine are called nontrinitarians, and their positions include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unitarianism">Unitarianism</a>, binitarianism and modalism; many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinitarians">Trinitarians</a> regard them as heretical.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/trinity.jpg" alt="Trinity" width="330" height="644" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Giovanni Battista Tiepolo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giovanni_Battista_Tiepolo_016.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The doctrine was formulated as early Christians tried to understand the relationship between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> and God in their scriptures and earlier traditions. Scholars cite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> passages that present a triadic understanding of God, among them Matthew 28:19, in which disciples are to baptise in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit, and Paul's blessing in 2 Corinthians 13:13. Larry Hurtado notes that the developed doctrine is not explicit in those books. Towards the end of the first century Clement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> asked in his epistle whether Christians did not have one God, one Christ and one Spirit, and the Didache directs baptism in the threefold name.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>In the fourth century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arianism">Arianism</a>, as traditionally understood, taught that the Father existed before the Son, who was a changeable creature granted the dignity of Son of God. In 325 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-council-of-nicaea">First Council of Nicaea</a> adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicene-creed">Nicene Creed</a>, which used the term homoousios, of one substance, for the relation of Father and Son. After more than fifty years of debate the term was accepted as the hallmark of orthodoxy and developed into the formula of three persons, one being. The First Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 381 expanded the creed to say that the Holy Spirit is worshipped and glorified together with the Father and the Son.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-of-nazianzus">Gregory of Nazianzus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-of-nyssa">Gregory of Nyssa</a> and Basil the Great held that the distinctions among the persons lay solely in their inner relations, so that there are not three gods but one divine Being in three persons. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustine-of-hippo">Augustine of Hippo</a> used a psychological analogy of memory, intelligence and will, and in De trinitate, written between 399 and 419, he described love as having three aspects: the lover, the beloved and the love. John of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, who died in 749, made perichoresis, the mutual indwelling of the persons, a technical term. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-council-of-the-lateran">Fourth Lateran Council</a> of 1215 declared that the Father begets, the Son is begotten and the Holy Spirit proceeds.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adoptionism">Adoptionism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arianism">Arianism</a> existed before the formal definition at the councils of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a>. Adoptionists held that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> became divine only at his baptism, resurrection or ascension. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> traditionally rejects the doctrine, although the Zohar of 1286 contains a passage some read as a kabbalistic trinity. The doctrine left marks on church building: many churches have three doors, and Gothic cathedrals used trefoils to represent it. In Christian art it is most often shown with the Spirit as a dove with wings outspread, though images with three human figures appear in most periods.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Trinity” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
